@module dart-m03 | Control Flow
@summary Make decisions and repeat work with if, switch, loops and Dart 3 patterns.

@lesson dart-m03-l01 | If and else | 12
## Learning objectives
- Write `if`, `else if` and `else` chains
- Combine conditions clearly
- Use guard clauses to reduce nesting

## Making decisions
`if` runs a block only when its condition is `true`. Add `else if` for more cases and `else` for everything else:

```dart
String grade(int score) {
  if (score >= 90) {
    return 'A';
  } else if (score >= 80) {
    return 'B';
  } else if (score >= 70) {
    return 'C';
  } else {
    return 'Keep practising';
  }
}

void main() {
  print(grade(95)); // A
  print(grade(72)); // C
  print(grade(40)); // Keep practising
}
```

Conditions are checked **top to bottom** and only the first matching branch runs. That's why the order matters: checking `score >= 70` first would give a 95 a "C".

## Braces
For a single statement you may omit braces, but Effective Dart only recommends this when the whole `if` fits on one line with no `else`:

```dart
if (items.isEmpty) return;      // fine
if (items.isEmpty)
  return;                       // avoid: easy to break when adding lines
```

## Guard clauses
Deeply nested `if` blocks are hard to read. Handle invalid cases early and return:

```dart
String checkout(List<String> cart, bool isLoggedIn) {
  if (!isLoggedIn) return 'Please sign in';
  if (cart.isEmpty) return 'Your cart is empty';
  return 'Checking out ${cart.length} items';
}
```

The "happy path" stays at the left edge of the function instead of hiding inside three levels of braces.

## if-case (Dart 3)
`if` can also match **patterns**, which you'll study in lesson 8:

```dart
void handle(Object json) {
  if (json case {'name': String name, 'age': int age}) {
    print('$name is $age');
  } else {
    print('Unexpected data');
  }
}
```

> TIP: When you compare one value against many constants, a `switch` is often clearer than a long `else if` chain.

:::check What does `grade(80)` return?
'B'. 80 is not >= 90, but it is >= 80, and the chain stops at the first match.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Writing `if (x = 5)` instead of `if (x == 5)`; Dart rejects it because assignment does not produce a bool for a non-bool x, but the intent was a comparison.
- Ordering overlapping conditions from least to most specific.

## Summary
- `if` / `else if` / `else` choose one branch, checked top to bottom.
- Use braces for multi-line bodies; guard clauses reduce nesting.
- `if (value case pattern)` matches patterns and binds variables.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Branches
