@lesson dart-m03-l02 | Switch statements | 12
## Learning objectives
- Use `switch` statements with constant and pattern cases
- Understand implicit `break` in Dart 3
- Combine cases and use `default`

## Choosing between many values
A `switch` statement compares one value against several cases:

```dart
void describe(String command) {
  switch (command) {
    case 'start':
      print('Starting...');
    case 'stop':
    case 'quit':
      print('Stopping');
    default:
      print('Unknown command: $command');
  }
}

void main() {
  describe('start');
  describe('quit');
  describe('jump');
}
```

In Dart 3, a non-empty case **does not fall through** to the next one, so you don't need `break` (you may still write it). Empty cases like `case 'stop':` share the body of the next case.

## Logical-or patterns
Instead of stacking empty cases, combine values with `||`:

```dart
switch (command) {
  case 'stop' || 'quit':
    print('Stopping');
  default:
    print('Other');
}
```

## Guards with when
Add a condition to a case with `when`:

```dart
void classify(int n) {
  switch (n) {
    case 0:
      print('zero');
    case int x when x < 0:
      print('negative');
    case int x when x.isEven:
      print('positive even');
    default:
      print('positive odd');
  }
}
```

## Exhaustiveness with enums
When switching on an enum (or a sealed class), Dart checks that every value is handled. If you add a new enum value later, the compiler points to every switch you forgot to update:

```dart
enum Level { beginner, intermediate, advanced }

String label(Level level) {
  switch (level) {
    case Level.beginner:
      return 'Start here';
    case Level.intermediate:
      return 'Keep going';
    case Level.advanced:
      return 'Expert mode';
  }
}
```

No `default` is needed, and adding one would hide future mistakes.

> TIP: Avoid `default` when switching over enums or sealed types; let the compiler enforce exhaustiveness.

:::check In Dart 3, does a non-empty case fall through to the next case?
No. Each non-empty case ends implicitly; only empty cases share the next body.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Adding `default` to enum switches, losing exhaustiveness checking.
- Expecting C-style fall-through between non-empty cases.

## Summary
- `switch` compares a value with constant or pattern cases.
- Dart 3 has no accidental fall-through; `break` is optional.
- Combine values with `||`, add conditions with `when`.
- Enum and sealed-type switches are checked for exhaustiveness.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Branches ▸ Switch statements
